00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:21.970
All right.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.280
Now Google Cloud
Platform is huge.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.894
We have a ton of
different products,

00:02:25.894 --> 00:02:27.810
and quite frankly, the
40 minutes that they've

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.580
given me here, we
don't have enough time

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.400
to go through all of them.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.590
So today we're going to
focus on three main things.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.160
Number one, we're going to lay
some foundation on the network

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.600
and show you how to save
a little bit of costs

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.290
there, just to kind
of ease everyone

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.540
into what we're talking about.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.470
Then we're going to
talk about some ways

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.150
to save costs on compute.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.590
And then we're going to go
through a couple of scenarios

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.880
that hopefully some
of you fall into

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.470
and you'll have a little
bit better time attaching

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.476
to your business model.

00:02:47.476 --> 00:02:49.350
But the most important
thing you need to know

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.110
is that nothing I'm talking
about today is secret.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.840
None of this requires a
handshake or the right Google

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.550
badge to get this
information about how

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.870
to optimize this stuff.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.820
Every piece of data
you're seeing today

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.820
is available for you to go
get right now, either using

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.060
the Google Cloud Platform
pricing calculator,

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.560
or looking at the pricing pages
for the individual products.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.350
Like this is it.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.894
Again, it's not an
Illuminati style thing here.

00:03:10.894 --> 00:03:12.810
You can go run the same
analysis on everything

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.980
you're doing on your own.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.530
So let's get started.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:17.282
Serving data.

00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:18.990
This is where we're
going to start today.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.930
Let's figure out
what does it cost

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.080
to serve one terabyte of
data from Google Cloud

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.810
Storage to your users.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.740
Just bare minimum
discussion here.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.500
Let's just talk
about serving data.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.142
Now when you think
about this, you

00:03:33.142 --> 00:03:34.350
have two components involved.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.327
First off is storage--

00:03:35.327 --> 00:03:36.660
where you're storing your data--

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.750
and second off is
your network costs

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.740
for actually
transferring the data.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.800
Now the costs for these
are dependent upon usage.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.630
So for example, Google
Cloud Storage, what you pay

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.580
is a function of
where you're hosting

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.539
your data alongside of
how much you're hosting.

00:03:52.539 --> 00:03:54.704
So for example, if you're
actually hosting your data

00:03:54.704 --> 00:03:57.450
in US central one region, you're
paying about $0.02 per gig

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.050
to actually store your data.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.200
However, you move to
US East, that changes.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.090
And if using a
multi-region bucket,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.950
then that changes as well.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.350
On the networking side, it's
a tiered cost structure.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.280
So if you're under one terabyte,
you pay a different price

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.530
than if you're in the next
tier of one terabyte to 10

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.630
terabytes, and 10 terabytes up.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.370
And then this also changes
per region as well.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.730
So for example, if
you're transferring data

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.100
from the Iowa location,
which is US central one,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.290
and you're in the first
tier of under one terabyte,

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.025
you're paying about $0.12 a gig.

00:04:28.025 --> 00:04:30.150
Now with this in mind,
though, we have to point out

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.900
something-- and again,
this is not secret,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.360
this is widely known--

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.120
is that there's a whole tier
of usage on Google Cloud that's

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.610
absolutely free that you get
every single month that you

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.080
can just use this stuff.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.670
No one cares about it.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.830
You're not going to
get charged for it.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.785
And it ranges from mostly
all of our products.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.660
But most specifically
for our case right now,

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.550
we're worried about,
what is the free tier

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.200
for network and storage?

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.660
On the storage side, we
actually get five gigabytes

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.150
of storage for free
each month, each region.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.500
So if you've got five gigs in US
East and give gigs in US West,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.290
each one of those is free.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:06.210
On the network side, we
get about one gigabyte

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.140
of egress, which
is outbound data

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.750
traffic, from North
American locations

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.500
to other destinations.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.260
And by the way, you will see
many caveats of little stars

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.760
during this, and you'll also
see a lot of little labels that

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.530
says, the price of today.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.310
This is the Cloud.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.930
Prices change.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:24.857
And if you discover this
video in the future,

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:26.440
you will undoubtedly
find that there's

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.620
a discrepancy in the prices.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:28.994
So please make
sure you check out

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:31.510
the documentation
for the latest.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.034
So this is our free tier.

00:05:33.034 --> 00:05:34.450
Let's talk about
egress cost then.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.590
So one terabytes of data
that we want to send.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:37.780
So what would this cost us?

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.210
So to store it, we get
about one terabyte of data

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:41.990
storing in US central one.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:45.370
That's 1024 gigs, minus our five
gig free tier at $0.02 per gig.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:46.660
About $20 a month.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.835
Just to store the data at rest.

00:05:48.835 --> 00:05:50.002
Not too bad.

00:05:50.002 --> 00:05:51.460
On the network
side, though, things

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:52.730
get a little bit trickier.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.313
So we've got our one
terabyte of data,

00:05:54.313 --> 00:05:56.470
minus the gig in the free
tier at $0.12 per gig.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:01.060
About $122 a month for
a total of about $143.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.500
So storing the
data, transferring

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.720
a terabyte of data, about $143.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.156
Which isn't too
bad I don't think.

00:06:07.156 --> 00:06:08.530
But the truth is
that most of you

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.240
actually don't send
your users directly

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.448
to your Cloud storage bucket.

00:06:11.448 --> 00:06:13.060
Chances are you have
some proxy, some

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.380
front-end that
they're hitting first

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.330
if you're website or something
else that this data is

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:17.420
being routed through.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.630
So let's take a look
at what that costs.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.020
Basically the same.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.050
So as long as you're
transferring information

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:25.180
in the same region
between a Cloud storage

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.807
bucket and a Compute
Engine instance, it's free.

00:06:27.807 --> 00:06:29.890
You don't have to worry
about that as long as it's

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:30.840
in the same region.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.720
And then your egress
rates are still the same

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.577
if they're coming from Compute
Engine or from Cloud storage.

00:06:35.577 --> 00:06:37.660
So basically, if you're
transferring all your data

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.910
through a proxy, you're
paying the same exact price

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.070
as you would if it was going
from Cloud storage directly.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:43.750
The only difference is that
you're now actually paying

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.666
for the Compute Engine
instance running there,

00:06:45.666 --> 00:06:48.967
or the Cloud function's instance
or the app engine instance.

00:06:48.967 --> 00:06:50.800
So basically, as long
as you're using proxy,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.907
the basic serving
costs don't change.

00:06:52.907 --> 00:06:54.740
But let's look at this
from a different way.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.260
When we serve data
to people, especially

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:58.510
through Cloud storage, one
of the things we care about

00:06:58.510 --> 00:06:59.720
is locality and latency.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.770
So we want to get our data as
close to our users as possible,

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.535
but we also want to do it
as cheaply as possible.

00:07:05.535 --> 00:07:08.030
So let's take a look
at that scenario.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.830
So let's say we're
serving our data here,

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.350
we've got it in US
West one, and we

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.690
want to serve it to a
user in US East one.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.240
So we're going across
the continental US.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.220
Price here is still
the same, $143.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.240
It doesn't matter where
the user is coming from,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.970
we still have the same cost at
rest and the same egress costs.

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:26.970
But we want to get that
data closer to the user.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.560
So what if we actually cloned
that data in US East one

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.460
and got it closer to the user?

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.850
Well of course at this point,
our egress is still $0.12.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:35.870
No change there.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:37.490
And Cloud storage
itself, we're now

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.890
duplicating the data between
two buckets so of course

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.254
we're paying that extra
$0.02 a gig to copy it

00:07:42.254 --> 00:07:43.670
into this other
location, so we're

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:45.230
doubling our cost for storage.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.270
And we also have
this extra $0.01

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:48.980
per gig transfer
cost between them

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.025
because we're moving our
data between regions.

00:07:51.025 --> 00:07:53.400
So now we actually have a cost
that's incurred with that.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.430
So to actually clone this
data between regions,

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:59.610
we're actually looking at $173.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.190
Not ideal, but it gets our
data a little bit closer.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.870
But the truth is we
might be thinking

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:04.590
about this the wrong way.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.450
So we do have these things
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.634
we have regional buckets
and multi-regional buckets.

00:08:08.634 --> 00:08:10.050
And what multi-regional
buckets do

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.800
is effectively duplicate your
data between regions in an area

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.079
so that you don't have to
do this cloning by yourself.

00:08:16.079 --> 00:08:18.120
The trick, though, is that
multi-regional buckets

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:19.300
cost a little bit more.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:20.307
So take a look at that.

00:08:20.307 --> 00:08:22.140
So if we want to go
from Cloud storage right

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.224
to our users, our egress
rate from a Cloud storage

00:08:24.224 --> 00:08:25.681
multi-regional
bucket-- by the way,

00:08:25.681 --> 00:08:27.450
there's going to be
lots of weird acronyms

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.707
and terminology in this talk.

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:30.540
It's labeled intermediate
so hopefully we're

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.840
all move in together--

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.570
$0.12 per gig egress.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.659
However, we're
paying $0.026 per gig

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:39.360
to store in a multi-regional
bucket, which puts us

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.370
at about $26 a month
for that storage

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:44.740
instead of what we were
paying for the single region.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:46.030
We're at about $149 there.

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:47.740
So not too much cost change.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.650
But the difference here is
that when a user in US East one

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.360
actually asks for
this asset, Google

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.980
handles the
duplication of getting

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.840
that data closer to the
user on your behalf,

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.740
and you don't have
to clone it yourself.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.010
So we basically just
saved an extra $30

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:01.870
by using a multi-regional
bucket per month

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.800
instead of actually
cloning it ourselves.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:04.300
OK.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:04.880
Sounds good.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.297
So we've actually reduced our
cost inside the regional US.

00:09:07.297 --> 00:09:08.963
But what if we want
to go international?

00:09:08.963 --> 00:09:11.680
So we want to serve this data
to someone in Europe, right?

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.150
Well let's take a look
at cloning there as well.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:17.990
So again, we have our clone data
in a US multi-region and an EU

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.380
multi-region, so obviously
we're duplicating our costs.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.850
We have the same amount of cost
egress for the EU multi-region,

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.586
which is selected
for these numbers.

00:09:26.586 --> 00:09:28.210
However, now because
we're transferring

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.830
data between multi regions
in different areas,

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.200
we're actually at
$0.12 per gig transfer.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.350
So we actually get a
little bit pricier there.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.660
$286 this is the result of this.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.640
So if you're trying to
get your data to users

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.470
in a different area
using the multi-region,

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.454
it's actually really expensive.

00:09:44.454 --> 00:09:45.620
But let's rethink this here.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.830
So the goal-- again,
we're shoestring.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.790
We want to make this
as cheap as possible.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.410
We want to get data from the
cheapest bucket we can store it

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.350
in to the farthest
away user as fast as

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.335
possible, as cheap as possible.

00:09:59.335 --> 00:10:00.210
We already have this.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:01.080
It's called the CDN.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:01.580
Right?

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.525
We've all been
working with these.

00:10:03.525 --> 00:10:05.400
So let's figure out what
the cost is of that.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.150
So we've got our standard
regional Cloud storage

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:10.670
in EU West at about $0.02 a gig.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.650
Now for CDNs, there's two
costs you need to be aware of.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.940
We have a fill cost
and an egress cost.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:20.090
To fill data into the cache,
you're looking at about $0.04

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.186
per gig to get it into the CDN.

00:10:22.186 --> 00:10:23.060
And that's worldwide.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.930
It doesn't matter
where you're going.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.470
So if you have data here
and someone in Nairobi

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.380
asks for your asset,
it's $0.04 per gig

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.660
to cache it to the
closest point of presence

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:33.920
that Google has there.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.180
Now egress from the CDN
is only $0.08 per gig.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.610
So when you combine these two
together, $0.04 and $0.08,

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.140
you actually end
up at about $0.12

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:44.810
per gig, which is your
standard outbound egress rate.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:47.900
However, this is assuming
a one to one ratio.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.120
So if you're actually
pushing a terabyte of fill

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:51.980
and serving that
same terabyte, you're

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.400
going to end up at about
$143 to get that asset as

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:58.350
close to every user in the world
without you lifting a finger,

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.470
except making sure your cache
headers are turned on properly.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.950
But in reality, this isn't
how things work out, right?

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:06.230
Typically the amount of
cache fill versus cache

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:07.890
serve are quite different.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.530
So if you're actually
in an 80-20 split

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:12.440
here where 20% of your data
is filled into the cache

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.650
and 80% is actually served
out, you actually only

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:17.570
end up closer to about $91.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:21.110
So as long as that ratio is
on the serving side instead

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.550
of the filling
side, you're going

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.730
to have a lot cheaper
representation and set up.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:28.040
You're going to get closer to
that shoestring value you want.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:29.540
So in general here,
if we're looking

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.760
to serve a terabyte of
data, the cheapest thing

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.400
we can do to get it to
users across the world

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.820
as fast as possible is simply
to set your caching headers.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.560
Any other tricks or
any other smartness

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.932
that you're trying to do
is going to blow that up.

00:11:41.932 --> 00:11:43.640
It's going to make
things more expensive.

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.160
So find the cheapest bucket
that's as close to you

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.266
as you can tolerate,
put your data in that,

00:11:48.266 --> 00:11:49.640
and then turn on
the CDN and like

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:53.140
Google's point of presence
network handle the rest of it.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.880
All right, let's
talk about Compute.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:58.249
So Google Cloud Platform has
a lot of Compute offerings,

00:11:58.249 --> 00:12:00.540
but today we're only going
to talk about three of them.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:01.998
First off is Google
Compute Engine,

00:12:01.998 --> 00:12:04.220
which is our standard VMs
that run in the Cloud.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.530
Second is, of course,
Google Cloud Functions,

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:09.620
which are our invocation
based Compute resources.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:12.260
And third, of course, is Google
App Engine, which has both

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.324
managed and invocation based.

00:12:14.324 --> 00:12:15.740
And for the sake
of this talk, I'm

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:19.070
going to ignore things like
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.890
and GAE Flex because
these actually

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:25.940
tend to price themselves
closer to what GCE is.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:27.902
But for the sake of
this 40 minute talk,

00:12:27.902 --> 00:12:29.610
we don't have to go
into all the nuances.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:30.570
It's a little bit weird.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:31.440
So I'm just going
to ignore those.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.773
We're going to focus on these
three offerings right now.

00:12:33.773 --> 00:12:36.207
So by cost, of course,
GCE is variable on config.

00:12:36.207 --> 00:12:38.540
Depending on what configuration
you're using is how much

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:39.550
you're going to pay.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:41.009
The beefier the
machine that you're

00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:42.800
using in the configuration,
the more you're

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:43.940
going to have to pay there.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.148
Cloud Functions, though,
it's a little bit different.

00:12:46.148 --> 00:12:49.520
Cloud Functions is about $0.40
per million calls that you make

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:50.190
to it.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:51.560
And then there's also
an additional cost

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:54.143
you have to be concerned with
is what we call gigabytes second

00:12:54.143 --> 00:12:55.280
and gigahertz second.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.520
And if you check out
the documentation,

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:57.436
there's a little bit--

00:12:57.436 --> 00:12:59.330
a little bit better
breakdown here.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.550
I'm not going to get a chance
to go through it today.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:05.300
And of course, App Engine is
going to be $0.05 to $0.30 per

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.370
instance hour, depending
on your config.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:10.190
So this is the default
cost that you have here.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:13.190
Now on the other side, we
have our free tiers, though.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.290
Now here's where
things get really cool.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:16.910
In Compute Engine,
if you're using

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:21.620
an F1 micro configuration,
it's free the whole month.

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:23.510
No cost to you.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.030
Free instance sitting in the
Cloud, doing your bidding,

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.520
as much as you'd
like it, all free.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:31.160
On GCF, you got about two
million invocations a month,

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:35.510
or 400,000 gigabyte seconds
and 200,000 gigahertz seconds.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.570
And on App Engine's, you
have 28 instance hours

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:42.680
a day for an F1 instance, and
about nine instance hours a day

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.141
for a B1 instance.

00:13:44.141 --> 00:13:45.140
And I know there's not--

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.760
I know there's more than 20--

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.590
there's less than
28 hours in a day.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:50.798
So that gives you a little
bit of wiggle room in case

00:13:50.798 --> 00:13:52.580
your extra instance
spins up in an F1.

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:57.650
So basically here when you look
at GCE F1 micro and GAE F1,

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:59.420
you kind of get
free compute as long

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:02.464
as you're under a
certain strata of usage.

00:14:02.464 --> 00:14:04.880
With this in mind, let's take
a look at an always-on cost.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:08.640
So let's say you want to run a
box 24/7 for an entire month.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:09.640
What does that cost you?

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.509
So months have different
days and we always have leap

00:14:12.509 --> 00:14:13.550
year and stuff like that.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:16.400
So I'm just going to assume 730
hours of consistent run time.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.850
What we end up with if
we're using an F1-micro,

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:19.990
it's free the whole month.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:21.350
Don't have to pay a thing.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:23.260
However, when you bump
up to an N1-standard-1

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:25.218
because you want a little
bit more performance,

00:14:25.218 --> 00:14:28.120
you start paying $24 a
month, and $194 a month

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.650
for an eight core machine.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.460
This is all on the
Cloud pricing page.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:32.870
You can see all of this.

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.470
And by the way, this is after
a 30% sustained usage discount,

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.194
which I'll get to in a second.

00:14:37.194 --> 00:14:38.860
Cloud Functions,
though, we can't really

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:40.030
compare apples to apples.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:41.930
Because it's an
invocation based system,

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.210
we can't just say,
consistently running.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:46.690
So what I've done here is
I'm making the assumption

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.910
that you're getting one
invocation to your Cloud

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:51.680
Function service every
second for the entire month.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.694
So you got about 2.6
million invocations a month

00:14:53.694 --> 00:14:54.610
that's going on there.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.260
That lets us kind
of simulate what

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.090
an always-on cost would be.

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:01.770
For this, it's about
$3.51 to basically

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:04.080
have a GCF function
running every second

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.290
for an entire month.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:08.930
On App Engine, you end up,
of course, in the F1 config,

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.020
it's free.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.640
Don't have to change
anything there.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.926
However, if that second F1
instance actually spins up,

00:15:14.926 --> 00:15:16.550
you get a little
spike in your workload

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:18.920
or all of a sudden
you're the top of hacker

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:20.570
news for some
reason, you're going

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:24.080
to pay $36 for that second
instance to come onboard.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.540
Of course, when you move
up to an F2 or an F4, which

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:29.740
again, are beefier machines,
you get the $73 and the $146.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:31.250
So basically across
the board here

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:36.660
you can see that in GCE and GAE,
as you get a stronger machine,

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.810
of course the costs go
up, while GCF is still

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.306
pretty instance based.

00:15:41.306 --> 00:15:42.680
Now here's the
interesting thing.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.000
So those are basic costs.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.700
So obviously Compute Engine
is the most versatile thing

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:47.240
we have.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:48.560
It can run whatever
code you want,

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.180
whatever instance you
want, your images,

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:51.180
and all this other stuff.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:52.850
But the problem is it's
also the most expensive

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:53.820
for what you're using.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.861
So it would be really cool
if we could figure out

00:15:55.861 --> 00:15:59.540
how to make that as cost
effective as our other services

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:01.530
without having some of
the same limitations.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.144
So for example, if we just
wanted to run our code on GCE,

00:16:04.144 --> 00:16:06.560
we have problem which is that
each function invocation has

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.814
a nine minute timeout.

00:16:07.814 --> 00:16:09.230
So you can't
actually get anything

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.250
for more than nine minutes.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:11.625
On the other side,
on App Engine,

00:16:11.625 --> 00:16:14.000
you have a 60 second
time out on requests,

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.459
but if you push
that off to a task,

00:16:15.459 --> 00:16:17.708
later you're going to end
up with the same nine minute

00:16:17.708 --> 00:16:18.266
timeout.

00:16:18.266 --> 00:16:19.640
So our goal here
is to figure out

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.820
how to make GCE as cheap
as our other alternatives.

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:25.370
And the good news
is we can do this.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:27.650
First off, let's take a
look at the GCE discounts.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:29.316
Now these are the
things that you really

00:16:29.316 --> 00:16:31.610
don't have to lift
any fingers to do

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.020
to actually turn this stuff on.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:34.550
So first off is sustained use.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:37.790
So what we'll do on Google Cloud
Platform is analyze your usage

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:39.987
and if you have a sustained
usage, so you're always

00:16:39.987 --> 00:16:41.570
on for a certain
amount of time, we'll

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.940
give you a default
discount for just

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.550
running your instance
with consistency

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:49.640
across the entire month.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.460
If you're willing to make a
phone call to our sales team

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:55.400
and can actually commit to
usage, specific usage, and say,

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:58.940
I'm going to run at least
2,000 hours this month,

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.082
or 730 hours this month.

00:17:00.082 --> 00:17:02.540
You know, it doesn't matter
what configuration you're using

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:04.609
and you don't have
any pre-definition

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:07.579
of specific upfront
fees or whatever.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:10.190
You can actually get
up to a 56% discount

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:12.329
by just making a phone
call to talk to sales.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:13.190
So if you're a
startup and you're

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:15.410
starting to see a little
bit more usage and you need

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:16.819
that dedicated
resource and you know

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:18.527
you're going to have
one or two instances

00:17:18.527 --> 00:17:20.839
running at a dedicated
fashion, making that phone call

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:24.650
and getting that 56% savings
is actually pretty huge.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:25.970
And of course, right sizing.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:27.803
So what will happen is
Google Cloud Platform

00:17:27.803 --> 00:17:31.346
will actually analyze the
usage of your GCE instances

00:17:31.346 --> 00:17:32.720
and look at how
you're using them

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.430
and actually tell
you whether or not

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:36.470
you're over provisioned
or under provisioned.

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:39.170
And you'll see a little icon
appear on the home page that

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:41.150
will say, hey, if
you click here,

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:44.660
we'll reconfigure this for you
and save you money every month.

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.270
Likewise it'll say, hey,
you're using this pretty hard.

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:49.700
If you click here, will
beef up your machine

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.175
so that your users
get lower latency.

00:17:52.175 --> 00:17:54.050
So it's a fun little
button that you can just

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:56.310
click, the deployment
happens behind the scenes,

00:17:56.310 --> 00:17:57.560
you don't have to do anything.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.600
So this is kind of
automatic stuff.

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:02.330
However, something
that's a little bit more

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.280
drastic in terms of
saving that does require

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:05.360
a little bit of
work on your part

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:06.830
is something called preemptive.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:08.990
Now what preemptive
VMs are means

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:13.370
that the scheduler is allowed to
kill your instance when we need

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:15.440
the resources in Google Cloud.

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:18.350
Now that may sound really
scary, but here's the fun part.

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:21.080
It will actually
alert your instance

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.680
before it's about to get killed.

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:24.740
So it'll send you a message and
your instance will say, hey,

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:25.910
I'm about to terminate you.

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:27.440
You've got roughly
about 30 seconds

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.620
to backup any data to
some persistent resource.

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:32.240
And then once it
kills your instance,

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:34.294
it'll start your
instance back up later

00:18:34.294 --> 00:18:35.960
and then you can then
go fetch that data

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:37.790
and continue working on.

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:41.807
So if you allow us to kill your
instance when we need the time,

00:18:41.807 --> 00:18:43.640
we actually give you a
substantial discount.

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:45.920
If you're running at an
N1-standard that's preemptive,

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:47.630
instead of paying
the $24 a month,

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:49.640
you get a 3x discount
down to about $7

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:51.410
a month for the same
instance, and all

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:54.290
you do is to have to write the
code so that you can lay over

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:57.500
those sorts of
terminations after time.

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.750
And the cool thing is that
you can actually put a load

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:01.020
balancer in front of that.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.840
So if you need scalable
compute and you

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.840
need the ability to allow
these things to die,

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.314
but because you've got so
much load coming in, you know,

00:19:08.314 --> 00:19:09.980
one of them going
down means another one

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:10.897
might have to spin up.

00:19:10.897 --> 00:19:13.480
Putting a load balancer in front
of your preemptive instances,

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.050
since load balancing
is free, again,

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:17.540
means you're only paying
about $7 a month per instance

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:18.770
that you're actually running.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:21.230
Of course, we can get this a
little bit cheaper, in fact,

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:24.050
if we actually use a micro
instance there instead.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:27.004
We can actually get it down
to $2.96 per month using

00:19:27.004 --> 00:19:29.420
an F1-micro, but because of
the fact that we have our load

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:31.795
balancer and because of the
fact that we've re-engineered

00:19:31.795 --> 00:19:34.670
our code to make sure that it
can be fault tolerant when it's

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:37.760
killed, we actually have a
massively scalable compute

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:40.430
resource that can handle
itself getting kicked over.

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:42.380
So if you've got large
amounts of workloads

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:44.005
or individual things,
you're processing

00:19:44.005 --> 00:19:46.640
lots of individual files
where something can pick up

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.010
processing 50% where
something else left off,

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:51.272
this is an extremely
cheap solution for you.

00:19:51.272 --> 00:19:52.730
Probably as cheap
as I was actually

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:55.035
able to find in my research.

00:19:55.035 --> 00:19:57.160
So in general, if you to
reduce your compute costs,

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:58.910
you got a couple of
things you need to do.

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:00.790
First off is
distribute workloads

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:06.850
that are based on invocations
to either App Engine or Cloud

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:07.660
Functions.

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.820
Those are going to be the
cheapest situations where

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.370
you can put your code
and have them run.

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:14.560
If you need something
long running,

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:16.150
then leave it back
on Compute Engine,

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.250
but otherwise try to
push as much as you can

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:19.120
to those resources.

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:21.220
That's going to be the
cheapest you can get.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:22.930
Likewise, make sure
that you're making

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.649
that phone call to sales.

00:20:24.649 --> 00:20:26.440
Let them know that
you're willing to commit

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:30.010
to a sustained use and get
that discount, because that's

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:32.680
going to be huge for them
work jobs that you actually

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:35.140
need running consistently
on Compute Engine.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.360
And likewise, if you
have scalable needs,

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:40.480
a load balancer plus a
micro preemptive instance

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:43.570
is pretty much the highest
flexibility, lowest cost

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:45.010
scenario you can be in.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.490
Having a bunch of little
spiders doing your work for you

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:52.390
at scale is just as effective
as having one behemoth doing it

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.710
for a higher price.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:55.210
All right.

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:57.090
So those are our
basics here, so we've

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.741
kind of laid the
groundwork for how

00:20:58.741 --> 00:21:00.240
we're going to
evaluate these things

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.573
and look at the big picture
between network and compute.

00:21:02.573 --> 00:21:05.040
Now let's talk about
some scenarios.

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.930
Personal privacy
matters online, right?

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:09.471
We all kind of care about that,
especially when we're logging

00:21:09.471 --> 00:21:10.890
into public WI-FI and whatnot.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:12.930
But sometimes I don't
like paying a subscription

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:16.680
to some other service
to handle a VPN service.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:20.310
So how cheap can we build
our own personal VPN service

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:22.050
on Google Cloud?

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:25.020
Now to be clear here, I know
that personal security online

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.532
is a very hectic area
right now, and there's

00:21:28.532 --> 00:21:29.490
lots of back and forth.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:31.489
So to be very clear, I'm
not going to talk today

00:21:31.489 --> 00:21:33.224
about why you need a VPN.

00:21:33.224 --> 00:21:35.140
I'm not going to debate
the different software

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:37.590
libraries you can use
or having your own VPN

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:38.955
versus being part of a cluster.

00:21:38.955 --> 00:21:41.580
I'm basically not going to talk
about anything that PR or legal

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.380
would raise an eyebrow about.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:44.880
But I am going to
talk about how you

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:48.410
can run your own VPN on Google
Cloud for as cheap as possible.

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:49.755
All right?

00:21:49.755 --> 00:21:51.630
So with this, we have
two components, Compute

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:52.129
and Network.

00:21:52.129 --> 00:21:52.760
Pretty easy.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.500
How a VPN generally works is you
have your client that connects

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.560
to some Compute Engine instance
that's running your VPN,

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:00.630
there's a socket
connection between the two

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:02.280
that are exchanging
encrypted data,

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.361
and then the Compute Engine
instance or the proxy

00:22:04.361 --> 00:22:06.360
will actually go out into
the fantastic internet

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:07.734
and do all your
fetching for you,

00:22:07.734 --> 00:22:09.420
return that data back
to your computer.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:10.980
This is a high
level approximation.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:12.987
If there's any security
engineers in the room,

00:22:12.987 --> 00:22:14.820
I apologize for
generalizing that to a point

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:16.979
that I can fit on a slide.

00:22:16.979 --> 00:22:18.270
So let's look at the free tier.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.070
Here how cheap
could we make this?

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:23.310
Well if we use our
instance as an F1-micro,

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.440
then we know we can
get that for free.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.650
And as long as our usage
is about less than one gig,

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:30.840
we know that we can
basically get that for free

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:31.980
as well, right?

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.140
So if we're using an
F1-micro and our usage

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:36.480
is less than one
gig, we can get a VPN

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.600
that we can use whenever we
want for free every single month

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.790
using Google Cloud Platform.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:45.090
And you only start to pay when
you go over that free gig.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:48.330
So if you're just using this
to log on to Wi-Fi hotspots

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:50.010
when you're traveling
around the world,

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:51.884
you basically get this
entire thing for free.

00:22:51.884 --> 00:22:54.240
No big deal.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:55.667
But here's one of
the problems is

00:22:55.667 --> 00:22:57.750
that if you're using a
preemptive version of that,

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:00.291
you have a problem that you need
to know where to connect to.

00:23:00.291 --> 00:23:03.600
And every time you reboot
that VM, you have a new IP.

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:05.280
Good news is on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:08.590
you can actually reserve
a static IP address,

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:13.560
and as long as it's assigned and
being used, it's free for you.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:16.590
So you can go in, create
your instance, let it run,

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:18.625
get a static reserved
IP address that you

00:23:18.625 --> 00:23:20.250
can use from your
personal devices that

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:24.150
stick around waiting for you,
and you don't pay anything.

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:25.890
So again, we can
run a personal VPN

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.960
with a static IP for
nothing each month.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:31.147
And again, if you eclipse
the one gig free tier,

00:23:31.147 --> 00:23:32.730
that's when you
actually start paying.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.530
Let's talk about two
specific scenarios here.

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:35.800
What if you want
better bandwidth?

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.140
So what if you're
traveling and you actually

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:38.850
want this VPN because
you need to stream

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.332
some video from some
different location, right?

00:23:41.332 --> 00:23:43.290
Well the important thing
you need to understand

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:46.780
is that the throughput
for a GCE instance

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:48.770
changes based upon
the core count.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:50.400
It's actually capped
at the core count.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:54.090
So the more cores you have-- you
can see here that on the side

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.070
there, we're at our
64 and 32 cores--

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:59.106
the more cores you have, the
higher your TCP throughput.

00:23:59.106 --> 00:24:00.480
And this is where
a lot of people

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:01.854
get caught up when
they're trying

00:24:01.854 --> 00:24:04.020
to increase their
bandwidth for their VPNs,

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:05.970
because you'll
notice that moving up

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:09.521
from the F1-micro, the next
available option is F1-small.

00:24:09.521 --> 00:24:11.520
Which you would want to
use because that's going

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:12.300
to be one of the cheapest.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.550
However you notice because
the number of core count

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:17.040
is the same, we actually
don't get any better bandwidth

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:20.160
by moving to there, even
though it's only $14 a month.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:21.300
Moving on to the next one.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:23.940
The actual first stage
you get where you actually

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:26.381
get some bandwidth increase
is actually the N1-standard.

00:24:26.381 --> 00:24:28.380
So unfortunately, if
you're moving up and trying

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:29.850
to get better bandwidth,
to even get it there,

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:32.690
you have to start paying $24
a month for your VPN instance.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:34.860
Unless of course it's
preemptive, in which case

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:36.390
you get it down to $7
a month, but then you

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:37.500
have to deal with
what happens when

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:38.875
you're in the
middle of a session

00:24:38.875 --> 00:24:40.470
and your VPN decides to die.

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:46.640
So let's talk about
something I deal with a lot.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:47.510
Traveling.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:49.217
So a lot of us are
international nomads.

00:24:49.217 --> 00:24:51.800
We spend a lot of time traveling
around, going to conferences,

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:53.174
talking to customers
and whatnot.

00:24:53.174 --> 00:24:55.160
It'd be really nice if
we had our VPN follow us

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.740
because if we boot up a
VPN and US central one

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:00.380
and yet we're in
Malaysia, we don't want

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:02.510
to wait for our packet to
go all the way to the US

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:04.130
and then come back to us
because that's just going

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:05.330
to make our experience bad.

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:08.167
So I actually put together
a little hack for this

00:25:08.167 --> 00:25:09.750
that I wanted to
share with everybody.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:11.480
So it turns out that
you can actually

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:15.750
run the Google Compute
Engine APIs from Google Cloud

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:16.700
Functions.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:18.740
So I actually wrote
a little web location

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:21.039
that I host that when I
load it while I'm traveling,

00:25:21.039 --> 00:25:22.580
it'll figure out
what my location is,

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:24.200
and in Google Cloud
Functions, it'll

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:26.240
figure out what
the closest zone is

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:27.860
that I can run an F1-instance.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:32.070
It'll kill whatever the
previous VPN instance I have is

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:34.940
and actually create a brand
new instance as close to me

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:36.520
as possible.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:38.200
So as I'm traveling,
I can basically

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:41.020
just click a single
button, kill the old VM,

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:42.520
and create the new
VM as close to me

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:44.680
as possible, no matter
where I am in the world.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:46.210
Again, for free,
because then I'm

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:48.100
not doing more than
two million invocations

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:49.460
of Cloud Functions per month.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:50.497
So this is really nice.

00:25:50.497 --> 00:25:53.080
So if you want to get the people
in your family using your VPN

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.020
and maybe charge
them $10 a month,

00:25:56.020 --> 00:26:00.650
this might be a nice little
racket to play at Thanksgiving.

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:01.150
All right.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:03.524
So let's talk about something
a little bit closer to home

00:26:03.524 --> 00:26:05.310
for a lot of you,
is WordPress site.

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:08.270
Anyone WordPress
experts in here?

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:09.040
Couple people.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:10.630
Still no caffeine in the house.

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:11.550
I like that.

00:26:11.550 --> 00:26:12.154
I like that.

00:26:12.154 --> 00:26:14.570
We're closer to 9:00 AM now
and everyone's still sleeping.

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:16.190
This is good.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.520
So WordPress is a large
and unwieldy beast, right?

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:20.770
There's lots of different
components involved and lots

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:22.270
of different ways
you can take this.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:25.589
So when you actually Google
for WordPress Google Cloud

00:26:25.589 --> 00:26:27.880
Platform, the first thing
you're actually introduced to

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:30.100
is a page for our
Google Cloud Launcher

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:32.680
with all of these pre-configured
operations that allow you

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:36.820
to one click deploy a WordPress
instance to your Google Cloud

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.657
account and just get
up and start moving.

00:26:38.657 --> 00:26:40.240
So you can click any
of these and they

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:41.906
have a bunch of
different configurations

00:26:41.906 --> 00:26:43.600
of how you want it all set up.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:45.340
However, the default
setup for this

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.254
is actually less than ideal
from an expense perspective.

00:26:48.254 --> 00:26:50.920
So these are all going to be set
up with an N1-standard-1, which

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:52.270
is $24 a month.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:54.760
And of course, you're still
paying that $0.12 outbound

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.037
egress from there.

00:26:56.037 --> 00:26:58.245
So this is putting us in
something we've seen before.

00:26:58.245 --> 00:27:00.340
But let's talk about
complexity here.

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:02.890
So what if you're using
the simplest WordPress

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:03.752
site possible?

00:27:03.752 --> 00:27:05.710
Well obviously you don't
need-- and you're only

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:07.420
using a single instance, right?

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:09.310
Because you don't
have that number--

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:10.880
that many number of users.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:13.300
Well, you can actually use
the F1-micro for this instead,

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:14.502
which is free per month.

00:27:14.502 --> 00:27:16.960
And again, if you're under one
gigabyte of outbound egress,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:18.849
you can actually get
that for free as well.

00:27:18.849 --> 00:27:20.890
So if you're a brand new
startup and your friends

00:27:20.890 --> 00:27:23.265
are all like, hey, go sign up
for this website over here,

00:27:23.265 --> 00:27:25.210
it's $10 a month to host
your WordPress site.

00:27:25.210 --> 00:27:29.560
And you're still live in on
ramen out of the VC incubator

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:32.080
along with 40 other
people, this actually

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.150
saves you that $10 a
month to buy more ramen.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:36.970
Yay.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:38.900
But the question
that we have then

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:41.860
is the concept that we're
free as long as we're

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:43.120
under the free tier.

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:44.869
And this is something
I've been harping on

00:27:44.869 --> 00:27:47.800
for a while is, what is the
cost before we eclipse that?

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:50.740
So if we assume that we've got
a network of one gigabyte free,

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:54.610
and our default WordPress site
that you deploy on App Engine

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:58.360
is about 272k per
visitor, it means

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:02.230
you can get roughly about 3,800
visitors a month before you

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:05.060
eclipse that bandwidth and
actually have to start paying.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:07.390
And this is a really
important number for startups,

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:10.390
for the hustle and
flow type people.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:12.640
Knowing how many
visitors you can actually

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:14.590
take before you have
to start paying money

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.640
and where you want to start
optimizing all of that data.

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:19.210
And of course, we could go into
a massive diatribe about how

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:21.520
to properly optimize your images
and compress your headers,

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:22.510
and serve all that data.

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:23.140
That's a different talk.

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:25.120
Right now we're just talking
about the fundamentals.

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:26.860
So right here we
basically say if we're

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.110
under roughly about
4,000 users a month,

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:31.755
we should be OK and
always in the free tier.

00:28:31.755 --> 00:28:33.880
And of course, if you turn
on caching headers, that

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.440
will put things
going into the CDN

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:37.330
so it will increase this
number significantly

00:28:37.330 --> 00:28:39.445
because the more users you
have that hit the same content,

00:28:39.445 --> 00:28:41.050
it doesn't come out
of your main bucket,

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:42.508
it comes out of a
different bucket,

00:28:42.508 --> 00:28:45.584
and you get a lot more users
to hit your information.

00:28:45.584 --> 00:28:47.750
But what if you actually
end up on the top of hacker

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:50.512
news suddenly and you need
to start scaling up and down?

00:28:50.512 --> 00:28:52.220
Well, the cool thing
here is that putting

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:55.340
the load balancer between the
CDN actually lets you do this.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:57.980
You end up with the same
egress, $0.08 per gig outbound;

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:00.350
and the same fill,
$0.04 per gig fill.

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.636
And we can actually end
up with the F1-micro.

00:29:02.636 --> 00:29:04.010
The problem now
we have though is

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:05.885
because we have a number
of instances instead

00:29:05.885 --> 00:29:09.530
of a single one, you
need a persistent MySQL

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:11.840
instance for each of
your Compute instances

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.340
to be able to sync between.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:16.280
Now unfortunately, as
of today I could not

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.550
find a way to get a free tier
setup with the SQL instance.

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:23.190
So that's going to be
around $29 a month.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:25.850
So if you're above
that 4,000 user mark

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:28.490
and you're expecting
scaling, the minimum cost

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:30.680
you're going to end up
paying is around $29

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:32.510
just to make sure
that that Cloud SQL

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:33.635
instance is sitting around.

00:29:33.635 --> 00:29:36.320
Or you could run it on your
own and N1-standard or F1-micro

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:38.870
and deal with all of the
provisioning and everything

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:40.480
else yourself.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.479
Now it's worth noting
here that there's also

00:29:42.479 --> 00:29:43.520
a plug-in for App Engine.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:45.177
So if you're running
WordPress and you

00:29:45.177 --> 00:29:47.510
want to run it on App Engine
instead of dealing with all

00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:48.926
that other stuff,
you can actually

00:29:48.926 --> 00:29:53.420
run it on an F1-micro instance--
or F1 instance on App Engine

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.620
and get that down to about
zero dollars a month.

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.520
Unfortunately, you still need
the SQL scalable back-end,

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:01.860
which runs on its own instance
at about $29 a month as well.

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:04.130
So something to think about.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.722
In general, if you're doing
WordPress on Google Cloud

00:30:06.722 --> 00:30:08.180
Platform, I got to
tell you there's

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:09.680
three strata you
need to think about

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:13.430
to make sure you're really
thinking about your shoestring

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:14.210
style budget.

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:18.230
If you're under 4,000 visitors,
just to use an F1-micro

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:19.970
and turn on the CDN
and you'll basically

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:20.950
get the whole thing for free.

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:21.830
Which is fantastic, right?

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:23.121
That's where we all want to be.

00:30:23.121 --> 00:30:25.280
If you're above 4,000
visitors, then you

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.830
need to start thinking
about stringing together

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:29.480
Compute Engine plus
the load balancer,

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:31.490
plus the CDN, and
plus the SQL option;

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.260
or using App Engine
to do the same.

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:34.790
Because you have
enough users there

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:37.040
you need to start making
sure that you're doing things

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:37.920
properly.

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:39.419
However I got to
tell you that there

00:30:39.419 --> 00:30:42.680
is a number of hosted
WordPress companies

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:46.760
that have their back-ends built
on Google Cloud Platform that

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:48.700
do offer you a
subscription based service.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:51.597
So paying $4 a month, they'll
host your WordPress site,

00:30:51.597 --> 00:30:53.180
they'll deal with
all the scalability,

00:30:53.180 --> 00:30:55.010
they'll deal with all
the security updates,

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:56.890
they'll make sure everything
is running in the background.

00:30:56.890 --> 00:30:58.070
You don't have to lift a finger.

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.736
And it might be a
cheaper option for you

00:30:59.736 --> 00:31:02.540
to fall into that bucket if
you're above 4,000 visitors.

00:31:02.540 --> 00:31:05.770
So that might be
something to look at too.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:06.367
All right.

00:31:06.367 --> 00:31:07.450
A final topic for the day.

00:31:07.450 --> 00:31:09.730
Video content site on
a shoestring budget.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:11.920
So with these things
in mind, we have

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:16.210
to look at what it would take
to make an education website

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:18.220
or like an online mook.

00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:19.960
You have students
that log in, they

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:22.990
have some data they want to
look at they, watch some videos,

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:24.430
they take some tests.

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:26.782
You know, maybe you
have maybe a sports site

00:31:26.782 --> 00:31:28.990
that's streaming all of your
high school sports games

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:30.580
and you got a lot of video
that you need to transfer.

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:33.100
But you have some combination
of rich multimedia

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:35.020
and rich website presence.

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:37.150
So with a standard
layout here, you

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:39.820
would probably have to store
that video data in Google Cloud

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:42.130
Storage and serve
that right to the user

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:44.110
so that you can get
streaming capability.

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:45.859
You don't want that
going through a proxy,

00:31:45.859 --> 00:31:47.986
you want that coming
straight to the source there.

00:31:47.986 --> 00:31:49.360
On the website
side, you probably

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:51.730
have an Apache instance or
maybe a WordPress instance

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:53.560
that is actually
hosting your website.

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:56.380
It's got some connection
to some relational database

00:31:56.380 --> 00:31:58.270
so we can keep track
of who the users are,

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:01.060
what data they've seen, and any
other keywords that you need.

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:02.890
And then of course, you've got
your standard load balancing

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:05.265
and CDN in front of that so
you can scale up, scale down,

00:32:05.265 --> 00:32:07.180
and make sure that your
data is consistently

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:09.310
sent to users around the world.

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:12.100
This gets pretty
pricey, unfortunately.

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:15.080
You're looking at $24 for the
Compute engines per instance

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:15.580
a month.

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:18.052
You're looking at $29 for
the Cloud SQL instance.

00:32:18.052 --> 00:32:20.260
Of course you've got your
fill and your egress costs.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.900
But where you really
get hit here of course

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:24.160
is serving that video.

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:25.402
Now as of today--

00:32:25.402 --> 00:32:27.610
and again, this can change
in the future-- internet--

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:30.250
if you're watching this
sometime in the future.

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:33.880
As of today, the
Cloud CDN that's

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:38.260
coming from Google Cloud Storage
does not cache 206 responses

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:39.670
from an HTTP header.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:43.720
206 responses are the ones that
are used for streaming video.

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:45.340
Which means as you're
streaming video,

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:47.740
it's not going
through the cache.

00:32:47.740 --> 00:32:49.400
So we have to be very
cautious of this,

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.800
which means we're
going basically

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:54.460
into the $0.12 per gig ratio if
we're streaming data directly

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:55.780
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:56.920
Not really ideal.

00:32:56.920 --> 00:32:58.750
This whole thing can
get pretty costly.

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:00.640
But there actually is
a solution if you're

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.139
willing to make a
couple of changes.

00:33:02.139 --> 00:33:04.972
We can get it's almost down to
free instead of all this cost.

00:33:04.972 --> 00:33:07.180
So first off, if you're
willing to make some changes,

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:09.790
leave your CDN in
place but instead

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:13.690
of actually using a dedicated
Compute Engine instance,

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:16.390
switch over your page serving
to be something invocation based

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:17.549
like Cloud Functions.

00:33:17.549 --> 00:33:19.840
Minimize all of your crazy
complexity down to something

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:21.742
that he can use a template
for to just scrape

00:33:21.742 --> 00:33:23.200
the data together,
put it together,

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:25.409
send it down in the HTTP
request as easy as possible.

00:33:25.409 --> 00:33:27.241
Make it invocation based
so you don't really

00:33:27.241 --> 00:33:29.170
need to do that much
in the back-end.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:33.250
And as long as you're under
two million page views a month,

00:33:33.250 --> 00:33:35.500
you can basically get that
entire serving process

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:36.800
for free.

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:38.110
Which is pretty nice.

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:40.360
Likewise, instead
of using Cloud SQL,

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:42.970
try to use Cloud Datastore
for your relational database.

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:44.410
If you're on a
shoestring budget,

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:45.940
Datastore will still
give you the performance

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:47.650
you need because most of
it is fetch based if you

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:49.030
are using a website like this.

00:33:49.030 --> 00:33:51.010
And it has its own free tier.

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:52.960
If you're under one
gigabyte of stored data

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:55.720
and you're doing less than
500,000 reads and 100,000

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:58.660
writes per month, you
basically pay nothing.

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:01.690
Which I think is right in the
range of a MOOC-style startup

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.190
because you don't really have
all the visitors you need yet.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:06.130
And even successful
ones are probably not

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:09.190
doing 500,000 or
100,000 per month.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:10.810
And a gigabyte of
relational data

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:13.310
is actually a lot
in that scenario.

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:15.489
So we can basically get
our entire web site serving

00:34:15.489 --> 00:34:17.679
and back-end storage
for about $0.

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:19.270
Now the problem,
though, is what do

00:34:19.270 --> 00:34:20.962
we do about the video content?

00:34:20.962 --> 00:34:22.420
Because there's no
real other place

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:24.295
to store this in Google
Cloud Storage because

00:34:24.295 --> 00:34:27.040
of 206 responses.

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:30.717
My only solution here,
a little bit of a hack,

00:34:30.717 --> 00:34:32.050
store all your video in YouTube.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:39.840
Upload your video to YouTube,
leave it is unlisted,

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:44.130
and basically when you're
streaming your embedded URLs

00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:48.210
down to your user, obfuscate
the hell out of your code

00:34:48.210 --> 00:34:51.790
so they can't figure out
where the video URLs are.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:53.710
If you can set it
up this way, you

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:56.560
get it entire content serving
and video production platform

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:59.350
for free.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:00.430
A lot of chuckles, yes.

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:03.100
This slide is very
contentious internally.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.470
We had a lot of talks--

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:09.520
we had a lot of talks
internally about

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:11.560
whether or not that
last little animation

00:35:11.560 --> 00:35:14.210
should be in this talk at all.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.580
So with that, I want to thank
you for your time today.

00:35:16.580 --> 00:35:18.400
I want to thank you
for being the hustlers

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:22.510
and the grinders working on
your costs every single day.

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:25.000
I want to thank you for
waking up this early to hear

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:26.710
another bald man rant at you.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:28.636
Most importantly, if
you care about cost,

00:35:28.636 --> 00:35:30.010
if you care about
the performance

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.870
your Cloud applications,
I got to encourage

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:35.200
you to check out our YouTube
channel for Google Cloud

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:35.890
Platform.

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:38.650
We have a ton of amazing people
working on amazing content

00:35:38.650 --> 00:35:41.020
to help you lower your costs,
increase your scalability,

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:43.228
and basically get your Cloud
application to where you

00:35:43.228 --> 00:35:44.560
want it as cheap as possible.

00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:45.760
With that, thank you.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:48.069
Stay sunscreened, stay hydrated.

00:35:48.069 --> 00:35:50.360
Enjoy your last day of Google
I/O. Thank you very much.

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:59.654
